    vec![
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "kv-dequant-materialization-batch-threshold-gate".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "A materialization pass that converts a compact cached operand (a quantized KV cache) into a wide form (f16) for a consumer kernel pays a FULL COPY of the operand, and whether that copy pays is an AMORTIZATION question: the wide form speeds every consumer read, but only enough query rows make the one-time conversion cheaper than the aggregate read penalty -- so the conversion must be gated on a batch/length threshold, with the gate REFUSING the materialization below it (the source: KV length ne[1] < 32 keeps the quantized cache and the attention kernel consumes it directly). Companion discipline from the same commit: the wide-form scratch buffer is RESERVED unconditionally even when the gate routes to the quantized path -- conditional reservation makes the graph allocator re-plan (reallocations) whenever the gate flips between batches, trading latency stability for a few bytes. The threshold is device-dependent (the source carries 'TODO: tune per device'), i.e. a tuned constant, not a law. Distinct from dequant-once-per-k-tile-into-shared-memory (in-kernel per-tile dequant -- no materialized copy exists to gate; this decides WHETHER the copy is created at all), from dequant-placement-by-component-traffic-share (WHERE a conversion sits by traffic share; this is a size-threshold ADMISSION for one conversion), from bind-now-materialize-behind-consumer-fence (defers WHEN a needed materialization happens; this can DECLINE it), and from shape-gated-warm-start-slot-copy (shape-gated copy of warm-start slots; the gate axis here is amortization by batch length, not shape matching). Source: PrismML-Eng/llama.cpp @ 5ea87dda (MIT) -- ggml-metal-ops.cpp:2809-2815 at the pin: '// depending on compute/bandwidth ratio, dequant to f16 kv is not always beneficial' + 'if (op->src[0]->ne[1] < 32) { return false; }' at the top of ggml_metal_op_flash_attn_ext_use_kv_f16; the companion at :2977-2982: '// note: always reserve the temp buffer to avoid graph reallocations' (the use_kv_f16 early return commented out in ggml_metal_op_flash_attn_ext_extra_kv_f16). Batch 128 / Plan 109 / Research 150. RIDER (Batch 129 sharpening facet + negative instances, ds4-metal @ b85a6174 -- qwen38-prefill-structural.md / qwen38-q2-speed docs): the amortization threshold has an EXPANSION-RATIO axis, not just a batch-length axis -- materializing a quantized operand to FP16 pays only when the byte-expansion ratio is small relative to the saved dequant ALU: the Q8 unpack won (+1.4-1.8%, ~1.9x expansion: 8.5bpw -> 16bpw) while the IQ2 gate/up unpack-once lost -6.95% and the routed-input pack-once lost -19.70% at the SAME 8K-token batch (~6-8x expansion from ~2-3bpw formats) -- 'the added traffic outweighed the saved work in the tested implementations'. This explains why the banked rule's threshold reads device-dependent: it is FORMAT-dependent too. RIDER (Batch 139 facet, ivanfioravanti/ds4-metal @ 18ca8ec MIT -- speed-bench/qwen38-m5-q4k-mpp.md + commit message): the same format-dependence extends to the PREFILL CHUNK WIDTH -- the chunk size that amortizes one format's dequant/staging costs is wrong for another: Q2_K is fastest at 8192-token chunks (~1064 t/s, +35% over the conventional 1024) while Q4_K is fastest at 4096 (~1000 t/s, 2.2x over 1024) and REGRESSES at 8192 (535-539 t/s); a chunk width swept once and shipped globally leaves the other format's campaign on the wrong arm. The knob is per-format-table material, not one constant. Round-identity facet: the unpack must compute the SAME rounding as the in-loop dequant ('Both paths compute half(float(q) * float(scale)), stage the same FP16 operands, and retain the same K accumulation order') or bit-parity breaks and the threshold is unmeasurable.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// Unconditional materialization: every attention call pays the KV\n// format conversion, even when the query batch cannot amortize it.\n// (std-only fixture: the graph allocator re-plans whenever the fast\n// path toggles between batches.)\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nstruct QuantizedKv {\n    rows: usize,\n    bytes_wide: usize,\n}\n\nstruct WideKv {\n    filled_rows: usize,\n}\n\nfn dequant_to_f16(kv: &QuantizedKv) -> WideKv {\n    WideKv {\n        filled_rows: kv.rows, // full copy of the cache on EVERY call\n    }\n}\n\nfn attention_prep_ref(kv: &QuantizedKv) -> WideKv {\n    dequant_to_f16(kv)\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// Amortization gate + unconditional scratch reservation.\n// std-only fixture: the reservation stays across both arms (no re-plan);\n// only the COPY is gated on the row threshold.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nconst MIN_KV_ROWS_FOR_F16: usize = 32; // device-dependent; retune per SKU\n\nstruct QuantizedKv {\n    rows: usize,\n    bytes_wide: usize,\n    payload: Vec<f32>, // the quantized cache, flat model\n}\n\nimpl QuantizedKv {\n    fn tiny() -> QuantizedKv {\n        QuantizedKv {\n            rows: 8,\n            bytes_wide: 8 * 64,\n            payload: (0..8 * 64).map(|i| (i % 7) as f32).collect(),\n        }\n    }\n    fn big() -> QuantizedKv {\n        QuantizedKv {\n            rows: 64,\n            bytes_wide: 64 * 64,\n            payload: (0..64 * 64).map(|i| (i % 7) as f32).collect(),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Graph {\n    reserved: usize,\n    wide: Vec<f32>,\n}\n\nimpl Graph {\n    fn new() -> Graph {\n        Graph {\n            reserved: 0,\n            wide: Vec::new(),\n        }\n    }\n    fn reserve_wide(&mut self, bytes: usize) {\n        self.reserved = bytes.max(self.reserved); // ALWAYS: no re-plan\n    }\n    fn wide_view(&mut self) -> &mut Vec<f32> {\n        &mut self.wide\n    }\n    fn wide_reserved(&self) -> bool {\n        self.reserved > 0\n    }\n    fn wide_filled(&self) -> bool {\n        !self.wide.is_empty()\n    }\n}\n\nfn use_f16_kv(kv: &QuantizedKv) -> bool {\n    kv.rows >= MIN_KV_ROWS_FOR_F16\n}\n\nfn attention_prep(kv: &QuantizedKv, scratch: &mut Graph) {\n    scratch.reserve_wide(kv.bytes_wide);\n    if use_f16_kv(kv) {\n        // dequant INTO the reserved wide view\n        let view = scratch.wide_view();\n        view.clear();\n        view.extend(kv.payload.iter().map(|v| v * 2.0));\n    } // else: the kernel consumes the quantized cache directly\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn small_batch_skips_the_copy_but_keeps_the_buffer() {\n    let kv = QuantizedKv::tiny(); // rows = 8 < 32\n    let mut g = Graph::new();\n    attention_prep(&kv, &mut g);\n    assert!(!g.wide_filled()); // no copy...\n    assert!(g.wide_reserved()); // ...but the reservation stayed\n    // a big batch on the SAME graph fills the same reservation: no re-plan\n    let big = QuantizedKv::big();\n    attention_prep(&big, &mut g);\n    assert!(g.wide_filled());\n    assert_eq!(g.reserved, big.bytes_wide);\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "offline-device-tuned-table-dispatch".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "Replace runtime autotune with an OFFLINE sweep per device SKU, emitted as a generated, do-not-hand-edit table keyed on (device, dtype, core dims, bucketed context dims) -> launch config, and make runtime dispatch a table LOOKUP with an explicit fallback ladder: exact key -> collapsed-domain default row -> family representative SKU -> baseline config. The bucketing is the load-bearing design move -- continuous context dimensions (the source: KV length ne11, batch ne01) are quantized into buckets so the per-device table stays FINITE, and the shortest-work bucket routes straight to baseline ('short KV: attention is a small slice of the step, left to baseline') because no tuning can help work that is a rounding error in the step. The table header is the regeneration contract: 'Generated by <tuner>; do not hand-edit. To retune or add a device, re-run the sweep and paste its output' -- the table is build ARTIFACT, not config. Tuner discipline travels with the table: pointwise no-harm gate on retunes, median timing per cell, thermal-drift cooldown re-measure before trusting a dirty window, and a device FAMILY folded to one representative SKU so the table covers untested siblings through the ladder's family arm. The source swept (Q, NE) for FA-vec across 5 device families: 'add 53 f16 (Q,NE) flash-attn vec instantiations (vec 80 -> 133)'. Distinct from autotune-aggregate-workload-key (keys the cache for RUNTIME-tuned results; this REPLACES runtime tuning with offline artifacts), from autotune-warps-stages (which knobs a sweep varies; this ships the swept results), from multi-priority-tune-groups (candidate ordering inside one tuning run), and from panic-safe-autotune-candidate (candidate failure isolation during the sweep). Source: PrismML-Eng/llama.cpp @ 5ea87dda (MIT) -- ggml-metal-tuning.cpp:65-68 at the pin: '// Generated by `ggml-metal-tuning fa-vec`; do not hand-edit.' + '// One row per kept bucket, plus per-(dtype,dk,dv) ne11-collapsed domain defaults' + '// ... To retune or add a device, re-run the sweep and paste its output.'; fa_vec_pick(device_id, gpu_family, dtype, dk, dv, ne11, ne01) at :1039 with the ladder 'exact bucket, then the ne01 domain default (ne11 collapsed); tried under each device tier' + '// family fallback: retry under the family's representative SKU; none -> baseline'; the short-KV arm at :1046-1049: 'if (ne11_b == 0) { return baseline; // short KV: attention is a small slice of the step, left to baseline }'. Batch 128 / Plan 109 / Research 150.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// One default for every device, or runtime autotune that re-sweeps\n// per process: either half the SKUs run a measured-wrong config, or\n// every first use pays the sweep again. (std-only fixture.)\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]\nstruct Cfg {\n    threads: u8,\n}\n\nimpl Cfg {\n    const DEFAULT: Cfg = Cfg { threads: 4 }; // measured 2x off on some SKUs\n}\n\nfn pick_cfg_ref() -> Cfg {\n    Cfg::DEFAULT // the device is IGNORED\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// Offline-swept table (generated; regenerate via the tuner tool).\n// std-only fixture: the ladder IS the decision content -- exact key,\n// then domain default, then family representative, then baseline.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]\nenum Device {\n    M3Max,\n    M5Max,\n    Unknown,\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]\nstruct Family(u8);\n\nimpl Family {\n    fn of(generation: u8) -> Family {\n        Family(generation)\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]\nstruct Shape {\n    dk: usize,\n    dv: usize,\n    ne11: usize, // KV length: short KV is a different bucket\n}\n\nimpl Shape {\n    fn fa(dk: usize, dv: usize, ne11: usize) -> Shape {\n        Shape { dk, dv, ne11 }\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]\nstruct Cfg {\n    threads: u8,\n    vec_width: u8,\n}\n\nimpl Cfg {\n    fn baseline(dk: usize, dv: usize) -> Cfg {\n        let _ = (dk, dv);\n        Cfg {\n            threads: 4,\n            vec_width: 4,\n        } // the known-safe form\n    }\n}\n\nconst FIRST_BUCKET: usize = 32;\n\n// context dims QUANTIZED into the bucket the sweep swept\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]\nstruct Key {\n    dev: Device,\n    dk_q: usize,\n    dv_q: usize,\n}\n\nfn key_bucketed(dev: Device, shape: &Shape) -> Key {\n    Key {\n        dev,\n        dk_q: shape.dk,\n        dv_q: shape.dv,\n    }\n}\n\n// Generated by `tuner fa-vec`; do not hand-edit.\nfn tuner_emitted_rows() -> Vec<(Key, Cfg)> {\n    vec![\n        (\n            Key {\n                dev: Device::M5Max,\n                dk_q: 64,\n                dv_q: 64,\n            },\n            Cfg {\n                threads: 6,\n                vec_width: 8,\n            },\n        ),\n        (\n            Key {\n                dev: Device::M3Max,\n                dk_q: 64,\n                dv_q: 64,\n            },\n            Cfg {\n                threads: 4,\n                vec_width: 8,\n            },\n        ),\n        // domain defaults: dk_q = 0 marks the per-device default row\n        (\n            Key {\n                dev: Device::M5Max,\n                dk_q: 0,\n                dv_q: 0,\n            },\n            Cfg {\n                threads: 5,\n                vec_width: 8,\n            },\n        ),\n        // family representative: Unknown stands in for the generation band\n        (\n            Key {\n                dev: Device::Unknown,\n                dk_q: 0,\n                dv_q: 0,\n            },\n            Cfg {\n                threads: 4,\n                vec_width: 4,\n            },\n        ),\n    ]\n}\n\nstruct Table {\n    rows: Vec<(Key, Cfg)>,\n}\n\nimpl Table {\n    fn exact(&self, key: Key) -> Option<Cfg> {\n        self.rows.iter().find(|(k, _)| *k == key).map(|(_, c)| *c)\n    }\n    fn domain_default(&self, dev: Device) -> Option<Cfg> {\n        self.rows\n            .iter()\n            .find(|(k, _)| k.dev == dev && k.dk_q == 0)\n            .map(|(_, c)| *c)\n    }\n    fn family_representative(&self, fam: Family) -> Option<Cfg> {\n        // one row per generation band; the fixture carries one band\n        let _ = fam.0;\n        self.rows\n            .iter()\n            .find(|(k, _)| k.dev == Device::Unknown)\n            .map(|(_, c)| *c)\n    }\n}\n\nfn pick_cfg(dev: Device, fam: Family, shape: Shape) -> Cfg {\n    let baseline = Cfg::baseline(shape.dk, shape.dv);\n    if shape.ne11 < FIRST_BUCKET {\n        return baseline; // short KV: a small slice of the step\n    }\n    let key = key_bucketed(dev, &shape);\n    let table = Table {\n        rows: tuner_emitted_rows(),\n    };\n    table\n        .exact(key)\n        .or_else(|| table.domain_default(dev))\n        .or_else(|| table.family_representative(fam))\n        .unwrap_or(baseline) // unknown everything -> baseline\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn unknown_device_falls_through_to_baseline() {\n    let cfg = pick_cfg(Device::Unknown, Family::of(3), Shape::fa(64, 64, 512));\n    assert_eq!(cfg, Cfg::baseline(64, 64)); // ladder terminated safely\n    // exact hit on a swept device\n    let hit = pick_cfg(Device::M5Max, Family::of(3), Shape::fa(64, 64, 512));\n    assert_eq!(\n        hit,\n        Cfg {\n            threads: 6,\n            vec_width: 8\n        }\n    );\n    // short KV skips the table entirely\n    let short = pick_cfg(Device::M5Max, Family::of(3), Shape::fa(64, 64, 16));\n    assert_eq!(short, Cfg::baseline(64, 64));\n    // an unswept shape on a swept device takes the DOMAIN DEFAULT row\n    let dd = pick_cfg(Device::M5Max, Family::of(3), Shape::fa(128, 128, 512));\n    assert_eq!(\n        dd,\n        Cfg {\n            threads: 5,\n            vec_width: 8\n        }\n    );\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "tuned-config-resource-limit-fallback".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "A tuned or override launch config must be re-validated against the DISPATCHING device's ACTUAL resource limits at dispatch time -- the table was swept on candidate SKUs and the box in front of you may differ (family-fallback sibling, OS/thermal limits, an override someone set) -- and on exceed the dispatch must FALL BACK to the known-safe baseline config and proceed, never error out and never half-clamp the config (a clamped half-config can still exceed the limit or silently change semantics). The source's guard sits between config selection and pipeline creation: shared-memory footprint recomputed for the TUNED queries-per-threadgroup vs the device's threadgroup memory limit, with the fallback being the baseline config (Q = 1) -- the form every device is known to fit. This is the dispatch-time twin of the offline table rule: the table says what is FAST, this check says what is POSSIBLE here, and the fallback composes the two without a failure path. Distinct from smem-envelope-gate-kernel-variant (gates VARIANT SELECTION on the smem envelope at pipeline choice; this is the fallback arm that fires AFTER a tuned config was selected), from panic-safe-autotune-candidate (isolates candidate failures DURING tuning; this validates the winner at dispatch), and from capability-gated-kernel-dispatch (probes whether a PATH exists at all; this assumes the path exists and bounds its resource shape on this box). Source: PrismML-Eng/llama.cpp @ 5ea87dda (MIT) -- ggml-metal-ops.cpp:3440-3444 at the pin: '// fall back to baseline (Q=1) if the tuned config exceeds threadgroup memory' + 'if ((size_t) FATTN_SMEM(nsg) > props_dev->max_theadgroup_memory_size) {' + 'cfg = ggml_metal_tuning::fa_vec_baseline_cfg((int) ne00, (int) ne20);' + 'nqptg = cfg.Q;  // = 1' (the device-prop name's 'theadgroup' spelling is the source's, quoted verbatim). Batch 128 / Plan 109 / Research 150.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// Trust the table: the tuned config was swept SOMEWHERE, and if it\n// does not fit THIS box the launch fails at submit time (or silently\n// degrades inside the driver). (std-only fixture.)\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nstruct Cfg {\n    smem_per_sg: usize,\n}\n\nstruct Args {\n    dk: usize,\n    dv: usize,\n}\n\nfn launch_expecting_fit_ref(tuned: Cfg, args: &Args) -> Result<Cfg, &'static str> {\n    let _ = args;\n    Ok(tuned) // no limit check on this device\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// Re-validate against THIS device's limit at dispatch; fall back to\n// the known-safe baseline on exceed. Never error, never half-clamp.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]\nstruct Cfg {\n    name: &'static str,\n    smem_per_sg: usize,\n    q: u8,\n}\n\nimpl Cfg {\n    fn q4() -> Cfg {\n        Cfg {\n            name: \"q4\",\n            smem_per_sg: 24 * 1024,\n            q: 4,\n        } // fit the SKU it was swept on\n    }\n    fn tuned_small() -> Cfg {\n        Cfg {\n            name: \"tuned-small\",\n            smem_per_sg: 8 * 1024,\n            q: 4,\n        }\n    }\n    fn baseline(_dk: usize, _dv: usize) -> Cfg {\n        Cfg {\n            name: \"baseline\",\n            smem_per_sg: 4 * 1024,\n            q: 1, // Q = 1: the known-safe form\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Args {\n    dk: usize,\n    dv: usize,\n}\n\nimpl Args {\n    fn huge() -> Args {\n        Args { dk: 576, dv: 576 }\n    }\n}\n\n// this box's limit (the swept box allowed more)\nfn device_max_threadgroup_mem() -> usize {\n    32 * 1024\n}\n\nfn smem_bytes(cfg: Cfg, nsg: usize) -> usize {\n    cfg.smem_per_sg * nsg\n}\n\nfn launch(tuned: Cfg, nsg: usize, args: &Args) -> Result<Cfg, &'static str> {\n    let cfg = if smem_bytes(tuned, nsg) > device_max_threadgroup_mem() {\n        Cfg::baseline(args.dk, args.dv)\n    } else {\n        tuned\n    };\n    launch_expecting_fit(cfg, args)\n}\n\nfn launch_expecting_fit(cfg: Cfg, _args: &Args) -> Result<Cfg, &'static str> {\n    Ok(cfg) // the limit gate ran: the fit is known\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn oversized_tuned_cfg_falls_back_to_baseline() {\n    let cfg = Cfg::q4(); // fit the SKU it was swept on...\n    let got = launch(cfg, 2, &Args::huge()); // ...not this box: 48k > 32k\n    assert!(got.is_ok()); // proceeded -- on the baseline config\n    assert_eq!(got.unwrap().name, \"baseline\"); // never error, never half-clamp\n    // a tuned config that FITS launches as tuned (no false fallback)\n    let fits = launch(Cfg::tuned_small(), 2, &Args::huge()); // 16k <= 32k\n    assert_eq!(fits.unwrap().name, \"tuned-small\");\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "quantize-scale-math-wide-precision-denorm-safe".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "DYNAMIC quantization arithmetic -- the amax reduction, the scale (amax/127), and especially the INVERSE scale (1/qd) -- must be computed in WIDE precision (fp32) even inside a narrow-precision kernel, because the scale can be a DENORM in fp16 (amax/127 underflows fp16's minimum normal ~6.1e-5 for small amax) and the inverse of a denorm OVERFLOWS to inf: every scaled value becomes inf/NaN and the quantized block is garbage. The fix direction is the OPPOSITE of the usual narrowing: the kernel's working type (fp16 in the FA paths) is WIDENED for exactly the four lines of the transform -- load in the kernel type, reduce amax, divide, invert, round in fp32 -- while the STORED result stays narrow (int8 payload; the block header's scale field is written from the fp32 value). The zero-guard stays (qd == 0 -> qd_inv = 0), but the guard alone was never the bug -- an all-zero row with a denorm scale still overflowed. No tolerance argument applies: this is a correctness axis (denorm/inverse overflow), not a precision-budget choice. Distinct from router-math-stays-fp32 (routing scores kept wide as a quality axis; this is the quantize transform inside a consumer kernel as a correctness axis), from per-block-int32-chunk-rescale-fp32-accum (accumulation-side exactness on tensor-core paths; this is quantization-side denorm safety), and from operand-precision-gated-by-consumer-invariance (picks the narrowest precision the consumer tolerates -- inapplicable here: the consumer tolerates NOTHING about inf). Source: PrismML-Eng/llama.cpp @ 5ea87dda (MIT) -- flash_attn.comp:129-130 at the pin: 'const float qd = amax / 127.0f;' + 'const float qd_inv = qd != 0.0f ? 1.0f / qd : 0.0f;' where the before-state typed all four transform lines with the kernel's FLOAT_TYPE (fp16); commit message: 'Codex found that qd could be a denorm and 1/qd would overflow.' Batch 128 / Plan 109 / Research 150.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// Narrow-precision transform: amax/127 can be a denorm in the narrow\n// format, and 1/qd then overflows its max -- every scaled value becomes\n// inf/NaN. (std-only fixture: the narrow format is an f32 carrier with\n// f16's exponent bounds; only the overflow class matters here.)\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nconst NARROW_MAX: f32 = 65504.0; // f16 max\n\nfn narrow_inv(qd: f32) -> f32 {\n    let inv = 1.0 / qd;\n    if inv > NARROW_MAX {\n        f32::INFINITY // the narrow store cannot hold the inverse\n    } else {\n        inv\n    }\n}\n\nfn quantize_row_ref(vals: &[f32]) -> (f32, Vec<f32>) {\n    let amax = vals.iter().fold(0.0f32, |m, v| m.max(v.abs()));\n    let qd = amax / 127.0; // DENORM territory for tiny amax\n    let qd_inv = narrow_inv(qd); // inverse of a denorm: overflow\n    (\n        qd,\n        vals.iter().map(|v| v * qd_inv).collect(), // garbage downstream\n    )\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// Wide transform, narrow store: fp32 amax/scale/inverse + round;\n// the payload and the header scale stay narrow.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nconst NARROW_MAX: f32 = 65504.0; // f16 max\nconst TINY_AMAX: f32 = 3.0e-6; // fp16-denorm territory for amax/127\n\n// the opt arm reduces in WIDE fp32: no denorm, no overflow\nfn quantize_row(vals: &[f32]) -> (f32, Vec<f32>) {\n    let amax = vals.iter().fold(0.0f32, |m, v| m.max(v.abs()));\n    let qd = amax / 127.0f32; // no denorm at fp32 ranges\n    let qd_inv = if qd != 0.0f32 { 1.0f32 / qd } else { 0.0f32 };\n    let rounded: Vec<f32> = vals.iter().map(|v| (v * qd_inv).round()).collect(); // transform in fp32...\n    let qd_narrow = if qd > NARROW_MAX {\n        f32::INFINITY\n    } else {\n        qd\n    }; // ...stored narrow\n    (qd_narrow, rounded)\n}\n\nfn dequant_all_finite(p: &(f32, Vec<f32>)) -> bool {\n    let (qd, vals) = p;\n    qd.is_finite() && vals.iter().all(|v| v.is_finite())\n}\n\nfn tiny_amax_row() -> Vec<f32> {\n    vec![\n        TINY_AMAX,\n        TINY_AMAX * 0.5,\n        -TINY_AMAX * 0.25,\n        0.0,\n    ]\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn denorm_scale_never_overflows() {\n    let vals = tiny_amax_row(); // amax ~ 3e-6: fp16-denorm territory\n    let p = quantize_row(&vals);\n    assert!(dequant_all_finite(&p)); // no inf, no NaN\n    // and the round-trip dequantizes to the original magnitudes\n    let (qd, q) = p;\n    let got: Vec<f32> = q.iter().map(|v| v * qd).collect();\n    for (g, w) in got.iter().zip(vals.iter()) {\n        assert!((g - w).abs() <= qd * 0.51, \"{g} vs {w}\");\n    }\n    // the naive narrow arm, same input: the inverse OVERFLOWS\n    let qd_n = TINY_AMAX / 127.0;\n    let inv = 1.0 / qd_n;\n    assert!(inv > 65504.0); // f16 cannot hold 1/qd -- the defect class\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "extent-clamped-operand-views-partial-k-tile".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "A tensor-API matmul whose op descriptor carries a STATIC tile K reads past the operand's K extent on the last partial tile (K % tile != 0): the operand views were constructed at the tile width as an extent, so the op trusts the extent and reads out of bounds -- undefined behavior (the source cites the MSL specification, section 2.22.2), and stale memory contents corrupt the result or produce NaN. The fix moves tail handling to the VIEW layer, three parts: (1) the op's K becomes dynamic_extent -- derived from the operand extents and edge-checked per tile, instead of a compile-time tile constant; (2) each operand VIEW's extent is CLAMPED to the remaining valid range per iteration (the source: kExt = min(N_MM_NK_TOTAL, K - loop_k)) -- clamp the VIEW EXTENT, not the data (no padding copies, no gather), and not indices inside the op's inner loop; (3) accept the degenerate-case cost honestly: on K-aligned inputs the clamp equals the full tile and the only overhead is 'a handful of integer ops per iteration' -- the aligned path is not special-cased back to the static form. Process note: the fixing commit carries 'Assisted-by: pi:llama.cpp/Qwen3.8-27B' -- a model-assisted kernel fix landed in the opponent's tree, the same model-assisted-fix posture this workspace runs. Distinct from pad-tail-to-fixed-tile (pads the DATA to tile size -- an allocation + copy this avoids by bounding the view), from block-count-clamp (clamps the GRID block count for partial tiles; this clamps per-iteration operand EXTENTS inside one block), and from clamp-tile-indices-to-sentinel-discard-at-write (clamps INDICES and discards at write time; this prevents the out-of-bounds READ at the view boundary instead of detecting it at write). Source: PrismML-Eng/llama.cpp @ 5ea87dda (MIT) -- kernels/mul_mm.metal:123-127 at the pin: '// Clamp the K extent of both operand tensors to the remaining valid K range so' + '// the dynamic-K op never reads past the K extent of src1 (or the staged A tile).' + 'const int kExt = min(N_MM_NK_TOTAL, K - loop_k);'; the descriptor at :71: 'NRB, NRA, static_cast<int>(dynamic_extent), false, true, true,'. Commit message: 'On the last, partial K tile (ne00 % 32 != 0) the src1 slice extends past the K extent of the tensor, and the op reads those out-of-bounds elements (undefined behavior per the MSL specification, section 2.22.2). Depending on stale memory contents, this corrupted the result or produced NaN.' Batch 128 / Plan 109 / Research 150.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// Static tile K in the descriptor + views built at full tile width:\n// the last partial tile reads past the operand's K extent (UB; stale\n// memory corrupts the result or NaNs). (std-only fixture: the OOB read\n// would be the bounds-checked panic class on the tail tile.)\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nstruct TensorRef {\n    rows: usize,\n    k: usize,\n    data: Vec<f32>,\n}\n\nfn view_full_tile(t: &TensorRef, k0: usize) -> &[f32] {\n    const K_TILE: usize = 32;\n    // extent FIXED at tile width: past the last row's K on the tail tile\n    &t.data[k0..k0 + K_TILE]\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// dynamic_extent op + per-iteration clamped operand views.\n// std-only fixture: integer-valued operands make every partial sum\n// EXACT, so the tiled checksum must equal the flat reference BITWISE,\n// and the clamped views make the tail tile panic-free by construction.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nconst K_TILE: usize = 32;\n\nstruct Tensor {\n    rows: usize,\n    k: usize,\n    data: Vec<f32>, // row-major [rows][k], integer-valued\n}\n\nimpl Tensor {\n    fn mk(rows: usize, k: usize) -> Tensor {\n        Tensor {\n            rows,\n            k,\n            data: (0..rows * k).map(|i| ((i * 7) % 5) as f32 - 2.0).collect(),\n        }\n    }\n    fn kn(k: usize, cols: usize) -> Tensor {\n        // B in a [rows][k] x [k][cols] GEMM: rows = K, k = cols\n        Tensor {\n            rows: k,\n            k: cols,\n            data: (0..k * cols).map(|i| ((i * 11) % 5) as f32 - 2.0).collect(),\n        }\n    }\n    // A's operand view: all rows, the k-window [k0, k0 + k_ext) -- CLAMPED\n    fn view_k_window(&self, k0: usize, k_ext: usize) -> Vec<f32> {\n        let mut out = Vec::with_capacity(self.rows * k_ext);\n        for r in 0..self.rows {\n            out.extend_from_slice(&self.data[r * self.k + k0..r * self.k + k0 + k_ext]);\n        }\n        out\n    }\n    // B's operand view: the row-window [r0, r0 + r_ext), all cols -- CLAMPED\n    fn view_rows(&self, r0: usize, r_ext: usize) -> Vec<f32> {\n        self.data[r0 * self.k..(r0 + r_ext) * self.k].to_vec()\n    }\n}\n\n// one k-chunk of the output checksum: sum over out[r, c] += av[r, i] * bv[i, c]\nfn tensor_matmul2d_dyn_k(av: &[f32], bv: &[f32], rows: usize, k_ext: usize, cols: usize) -> f32 {\n    let mut acc = 0.0f32;\n    for r in 0..rows {\n        for c in 0..cols {\n            for i in 0..k_ext {\n                acc += av[r * k_ext + i] * bv[i * cols + c];\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    acc\n}\n\nfn matmul_tiles(a: &Tensor, b: &Tensor) -> f32 {\n    let mut acc = 0.0f32;\n    let mut k0 = 0;\n    while k0 < a.k {\n        let k_ext = K_TILE.min(a.k - k0); // clamp the VIEW, not the data\n        let av = a.view_k_window(k0, k_ext);\n        let bv = b.view_rows(k0, k_ext);\n        acc += tensor_matmul2d_dyn_k(&av, &bv, a.rows, k_ext, b.k);\n        k0 += K_TILE;\n    }\n    acc\n}\n\nfn reference_gemm(a: &Tensor, b: &Tensor) -> f32 {\n    let mut acc = 0.0f32;\n    for r in 0..a.rows {\n        for c in 0..b.k {\n            for i in 0..a.k {\n                acc += a.data[r * a.k + i] * b.data[i * b.k + c];\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    acc\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn partial_tail_tile_never_reads_oob() {\n    let a = Tensor::mk(8, 70); // k = 70: 70 % 32 != 0\n    let b = Tensor::kn(70, 12);\n    let got = matmul_tiles(&a, &b);\n    assert!(!got.is_nan()); // the stale-memory NaN class is gone\n    assert_eq!(\n        got.to_bits(),\n        reference_gemm(&a, &b).to_bits() // exact: integer-valued operands\n    );\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
    ]
